{
 "id": "combination-sum",
 "title": "Combination Sum",
 "pattern": "backtracking",
 "fine": "Backtracking",
 "level": "medium",
 "statement": "<p>A cloud provider sells compute in fixed block sizes, for example 2, 3 and 5 vCPUs. You can buy any number of each block size. A team needs <b>exactly</b> <code>target</code> vCPUs and wants to see every way to get there.</p><p>Write <code>def solution(candidates, target)</code>. <code>candidates</code> holds <b>distinct</b> positive block sizes. Return every combination of block sizes (each size may be used any number of times) whose sum is <code>target</code>. A combination is a multiset: <code>[2, 3]</code> and <code>[3, 2]</code> are the same one, so list it once.</p><p>The order is fixed so the answer is unique: each combination is in non-decreasing order, and the list of combinations is sorted the way Python sorts lists. Return <code>[]</code> if there is none.</p>",
 "examples": [
  {
   "input": "candidates = [2, 3, 6, 7], target = 7",
   "output": "[[2, 2, 3], [7]]",
   "why": "The LeetCode 39 example 1. 2 is used twice in the first combination."
  },
  {
   "input": "candidates = [2, 3, 5], target = 8",
   "output": "[[2, 2, 2, 2], [2, 3, 3], [3, 5]]",
   "why": "The LeetCode 39 example 2. Listed in the required order."
  },
  {
   "input": "candidates = [2], target = 1",
   "output": "[]",
   "why": "The LeetCode 39 example 3. No block is as small as 1, so there is no combination."
  }
 ],
 "constraints": [
  "1 &le; len(candidates) &le; 30",
  "2 &le; candidates[i] &le; 50, all distinct, not necessarily sorted",
  "1 &le; target &le; 150",
  "the tests keep the answer below 1,000 combinations"
 ],
 "starter": "def solution(candidates, target):\n    # write your code here\n    pass\n",
 "think": [
  {
   "step": "Understand",
   "q": "What counts as one combination, and what stops you from listing it twice?",
   "a": "A combination is how many of each block size you buy. Order does not matter, so <code>[2, 2, 3]</code>, <code>[2, 3, 2]</code> and <code>[3, 2, 2]</code> are one combination. If you always write it in non-decreasing order, each combination has exactly one spelling, and you only need to generate that spelling."
  },
  {
   "step": "Small example by hand",
   "q": "Find all combinations for <code>candidates = [2, 3, 5]</code>, <code>target = 8</code>.",
   "a": "Write each combination in non-decreasing order and grow it from the left. Start with 2: <code>[2, 2, 2, 2]</code> works. <code>[2, 2]</code> leaves 4, and only 2 + 2 fits, which you already have. <code>[2, 3]</code> leaves 3: <code>[2, 3, 3]</code> works. <code>[2, 5]</code> leaves 1: no. Start with 3: <code>[3, 3]</code> leaves 2, but only sizes &ge; 3 may follow: no. <code>[3, 5]</code> works. Start with 5: 3 is left and only 5 may follow: no. Answer: <code>[[2, 2, 2, 2], [2, 3, 3], [3, 5]]</code>."
  },
  {
   "step": "Naive idea",
   "q": "What is the first idea that works?",
   "a": "Try every count for every size: 0 to <code>target // size</code> of each, with <code>itertools.product</code>, and keep the count vectors whose sum is <code>target</code>. It never gives up early, so with 8 sizes and target 150 it checks tens of millions of vectors."
  },
  {
   "step": "Which pattern?",
   "q": "Which pattern?",
   "a": "<b>Backtracking with pruning.</b> Sort the sizes. Build one combination left to right; the next size you add is never smaller than the last one, which kills duplicates. Track what is left of the target. If the next size is bigger than what is left, every later size is too (they are sorted), so stop the loop. The search is still exponential in the worst case, but it only walks prefixes that can still succeed."
  },
  {
   "step": "Plan in plain steps",
   "q": "Write the steps.",
   "a": "<ol><li>Sort <code>candidates</code>.</li><li><code>go(start, left)</code>: if <code>left == 0</code>, record a copy of <code>path</code> and return.</li><li>For <code>i</code> from <code>start</code>: if <code>candidates[i] &gt; left</code>, break. Otherwise append it, call <code>go(i, left - candidates[i])</code> (same <code>i</code>: it may be reused), and pop.</li><li>Call <code>go(0, target)</code> and return the list.</li></ol>"
  },
  {
   "step": "Edge cases to test",
   "q": "What could break?",
   "a": "No combination at all (<code>[]</code>). A target smaller than every size. A target equal to one size. Unsorted candidates. A single size that divides the target, or does not. Passing <code>i + 1</code> instead of <code>i</code> by mistake, which forbids reuse. Recursion depth is at most <code>target / smallest size</code>, which is at most 75 here."
  }
 ],
 "hints": [
  "Write every combination in non-decreasing order. Then each one has a single spelling, and you never need a set to remove duplicates.",
  "Build the combination one block at a time and keep what is left of the target. Only allow the next block to be the same size or bigger than the last.",
  "Sort the sizes. Recurse with the same index (reuse allowed). When a size is bigger than what is left, <code>break</code>: every later size is bigger still."
 ],
 "solution": "def solution(candidates, target):\n    sizes = sorted(candidates)\n    out = []\n    path = []\n\n    def go(start, left):\n        if left == 0:\n            out.append(path[:])          # a full combination; store a copy\n            return\n        for i in range(start, len(sizes)):\n            if sizes[i] > left:\n                break                    # sorted: every later size is too big as well\n            path.append(sizes[i])\n            go(i, left - sizes[i])       # i, not i + 1: the same size may repeat\n            path.pop()\n\n    go(0, target)                        # depth at most target // sizes[0]\n    return out\n",
 "brute": "from itertools import product\n\ndef solution(candidates, target):\n    # Try every count of every size, with no early exit.\n    # The number of count vectors grows like (target / size) ** len(candidates).\n    sizes = sorted(candidates)\n    out = []\n    for counts in product(*[range(target // s + 1) for s in sizes]):\n        if sum(c * s for c, s in zip(counts, sizes)) == target:\n            combo = []\n            for c, s in zip(counts, sizes):\n                combo += [s] * c\n            out.append(combo)\n    out.sort()\n    return out\n",
 "explain": "<p>Example 2, <code>candidates = [2, 3, 5]</code>, <code>target = 8</code>:</p><ul><li><code>go(0, 8)</code> adds 2 &rarr; <code>go(0, 6)</code> adds 2 &rarr; <code>go(0, 4)</code> adds 2 &rarr; <code>go(0, 2)</code> adds 2 &rarr; left 0: record <code>[2, 2, 2, 2]</code>.</li><li>In <code>go(0, 2)</code>, 3 &gt; 2: break. In <code>go(0, 4)</code>, try 3 (left 1: no size fits), then 5 &gt; 4: break.</li><li>In <code>go(0, 6)</code>, try 3 &rarr; <code>go(1, 3)</code> adds 3: record <code>[2, 3, 3]</code>. Then 5 leaves 1: nothing fits.</li><li>Back at the top, try 3 &rarr; <code>go(1, 5)</code>: 3 leaves 2, but only 3 and 5 may follow, so no; 5 leaves 0: record <code>[3, 5]</code>.</li><li>Finally 5 &rarr; <code>go(2, 3)</code>: 5 &gt; 3, break.</li></ul><p>Answer <b>[[2, 2, 2, 2], [2, 3, 3], [3, 5]]</b>. The records come out already sorted: smaller sizes are tried first, and no recorded combination is a prefix of another (both would sum to the target). The <code>break</code> is the pruning that makes backtracking far faster than trying every count.</p>",
 "complexity": "Exponential in the worst case (roughly O(k^(target / min size)) for k sizes), but pruning skips every prefix that overshoots; O(target / min size) recursion depth",
